Read-only diagnosis of "movement feels delayed" in b4. Nothing built. Short version: the app's own frame time (0.2-2 ms) cannot explain it, so the delay is between the wheel event and the picture on the panel. I found two code-level sources of delay and three more that need measuring on his PC. I can't tell yet which one dominates, so I propose one diagnostic build where he flips each suspect live and tells us which one fixes the feel.

FIRST, a correction to my own perf.log (explains the earlier puzzle): the "gap_max" column ignores any gap over 400 ms (my filter `gap < 400.0`, app.rs line ~951), so it hid the 430-510 ms frames. Those frame_max spikes were real UI-thread time. Most likely cause, from code: when scrolling pauses, the UI thread itself extracts text and link data for the current page (app.rs line ~517, `page_chars`/`page_links`, link resolving can be slow on a book with a table of contents), which is the idle prefetch I added. It matches "3 spikes in 23 s, only on pauses" and a dense page that also took ~500 ms to render. Unproven. He says he did not notice a hitch, but I will move that work to the render thread anyway.

WHAT ADDS DELAY BETWEEN INPUT AND SCREEN (ranked)
1. Wheel easing inside egui (VERIFIED in egui 0.34.3 source, input_state/wheel_state.rs): a mouse-wheel notch is not applied at once. It goes into `unprocessed_wheel_delta` and is released with an exponential ease (90% after 0.1 s, the tail longer). Touchpad deltas under 8 points skip it, wheel notches (Line unit) and big steps do not. So every notch starts moving on the next frame but takes ~100-250 ms to arrive. That reads as "floaty, delayed" and it is the same in every egui build, so it may be part of why it never felt like the Chromium viewer. Our code uses `smooth_scroll_delta`, i.e. this eased value (app.rs `self.scroll -= sd`).
2. Present chain (VERIFIED defaults: eframe 0.34.3 NativeOptions.vsync = true, glow backend, swap interval Wait(1)). In windowed OpenGL on Windows, the picture goes through the swap queue plus DWM composition; drivers also keep up to ~3 pre-rendered frames (NVIDIA "max pre-rendered frames" default; Intel similar). At 144 Hz that is ~14-21 ms, at 60 Hz ~50 ms. His gap_max 7-13 ms says the frame cadence is ~144 Hz, so this is probably a smaller part but not zero. (Assumed: driver queue depth on his machine.)
3. Which GPU draws the window (UNKNOWN). The UI is drawn with OpenGL through whichever GPU Windows gives litepdf.exe. He set "High performance" (3050) for OCR, which makes the 3050 render a window that is displayed through the Intel iGPU on most Optimus laptops: that adds a copy and often a frame of latency. The app does not log the GL renderer, so I can't say. This setting changed with b3's OCR advice, so it could also explain "laggier than the original".
4. Texture upload cost lands in the paint step (VERIFIED from structure): my perf.log "up" only times our CPU conversion, not the real OpenGL upload, which egui does after our update. Up to 12 MB (3 tiles of 4 MB) per frame can be uploaded; on Intel shared memory that can cost several ms and make a 6.9 ms frame (144 Hz) miss its slot. perf.log can't see it. (Assumed.)
5. Smaller: the perf.log write each second runs on the UI thread (disk/Defender can stall a frame); worker `request_repaint` after every tile adds extra frames (harmless but not free).
Pop-in (blurry then sharp) is not the problem he describes; he says it is the movement.

DIAGNOSIS PLAN (one diagnostic build, "b5-diag", all switchable, no rewrite)
Settings via a small "Diagnostics" menu (live) and launch flags (for things that need a restart):
 a. Wheel mode: [egui eased (current)] / [raw: apply each notch immediately, 0 ms easing] / [short 40 ms ease]. Live toggle. This isolates item 1 in 10 seconds of feel-testing.
 b. VSync on/off (--vsync=off at launch; shows tearing but removes queueing, isolates item 2). 
 c. "gl finish at start of frame" toggle: waits until the previous frame is done on the GPU so the driver can't queue frames (classic latency cap). Live. Isolates the queue part of item 2.
 d. Log GL_RENDERER / GL_VENDOR, the monitor refresh rate (Win32) and the Windows per-app GPU preference at start, so we know which GPU draws the window (item 3). I can also add a flag that writes the Windows graphics preference for litepdf.exe to "power saving" / "high performance" so he can A/B without opening Settings.
 e. Real upload cost: wrap egui's texture upload timing is not exposed, so I would reduce the per-frame upload budget to 1 tile and add a toggle; if the feel improves, it is item 4. 
 f. Move page text/link extraction to the render thread (fixes the 430-510 ms spikes regardless).
 g. Log fixes: remove the 400 ms gap filter, add a histogram of frame gaps (p50/p95/p99 and count above 1.5x refresh), add a line for any frame over 30 ms with per-stage timings (poll, panels, central, extraction, build_requests), add worker lines for renders over 150 ms (page, kind, time).
Input-to-photon needs a camera or PresentMon (can't be measured accurately inside the app): ask him to either film the screen at 240 fps (phone slow-mo) next to a mouse wheel click, or run PresentMon (free, "PresentMon-*.exe --process_name litepdf.exe --output_file pm.csv") for 20 s of scrolling and send the csv: it shows the present mode (e.g. "Composed: Copy with GPU GDI" vs "Hardware: Independent Flip"), ms between presents and ms until displayed. In-app I can add a wheel-to-applied-position log: time from the first wheel event until 90% / 99% of the requested scroll has been applied (theory: ~100 ms / ~200+ ms with egui easing).

PROPOSED FIXES, if the diagnostic points at them (not built; effort and size):
- Raw wheel handling (item 1): small change, 30 min, no size change. Likely the biggest win for "delayed". I would apply notches immediately with a very short (<=40 ms) ease and let touchpad deltas pass through directly.
- gl.finish / frame cap (item 2): small change, no size change.
- Switch the UI renderer to wgpu with DirectX 12 flip-model swap chain and frame latency 1 (items 2-3): the proper low-latency present path, like Chromium uses, but it adds a few MB (maybe +5-8 MB to the installer) and needs testing; only if a-c are not enough.
- Pin the UI to the GPU that owns the display (item 3): set the app's Windows GPU preference to power saving for the UI process while OCR runs in its own process with DirectML on the 3050 (OCR already runs as a child process, so they can use different GPUs). Cheap if confirmed by d.
- Upload budget to 1 tile per frame and tiles of 512 px (item 4).

What I need from him (answers only he has): mouse wheel or touchpad; laptop refresh rate; what Windows "Graphics settings" says for litepdf.exe now (Power saving / High performance / System default); which build he means by "the original" (b1, b2 or b3?). If he says go, I build b5-diag with a-g in about an hour and send it with the toggles.
